    "summary": "Pastoralist families in Somaliland's Marodi-Jeh region are stuck on barren land after migrating in search of pasture, with dying livestock and no funds to move on or return home.",
    "content": "Several nomadic families who left their home areas in search of better grazing conditions have become stranded in the dry plains south of Hargeisa, the capital of Somaliland. They moved their herds in April expecting seasonal rains, but the land has remained parched, leaving their animals weak and dying.\n\nOne such herder, Abdikarim Sulub Mohamed, relocated with his family and livestock from Ina-Guuha, north of Hargeisa, to the Qool-adey area in the Marodi-Jeh region. The move was intended to save their animals from the drought, but instead they found a landscape devoid of water and pasture.\n\nThe families now face a dire choice: they cannot trek back because their animals are too weak for another long journey, and they lack the money to hire trucks to transport themselves and their livestock to a more viable location. This has left them effectively trapped in the open plainland with no resources to improve their situation.\n\nThis crisis is part of a broader pattern of repeated droughts in the Horn of Africa, which have increasingly pushed pastoralists to migrate longer distances, often with little success. For the Somali diaspora, this highlights the urgent need for sustained support to vulnerable communities, as many diaspora members send remittances to relatives in such areas, but these funds may be insufficient for costly relocations or recovery efforts.\n\nThe situation also underscores the importance of early warning systems and resilience-building initiatives to help pastoralists adapt to changing climate patterns, rather than being forced into desperate last-resort migrations. As the drought continues, the stranded families in Qool-adey remain in urgent need of assistance, yet their location and lack of resources make them hard to reach.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/08/somaliland-pastoralists-stranded-on-a-dry-plainland-with-dying-animals-and-no-resources-to-move-2/",
